# Architecture principles

Write code that survives design churn and growth. Prefer clarity over cleverness.

## Core

| Principle | Meaning here |
|-----------|----------------|
| **SoC** | UI ≠ data fetching ≠ domain rules ≠ persistence. One module, one reason to change. |
| **DRY** | Share *real* duplication (schemas, UI primitives, auth). Do not abstract after one use. |
| **KISS** | Smallest design that works. No framework-inside-framework. |
| **YAGNI** | Do not build “future marketplace” / generic engines until the product needs them. |
| **SOLID** | Especially **S** (one responsibility) and **D** (depend on contracts/interfaces, not Nest/Prisma/React details in domain code). |

## Also required (often missed)

- **Composition over inheritance** — combine small units; avoid deep class/component trees
- **Explicit boundaries** — web talks to API only via `@nechto/api-client` + `@nechto/api-contract`
- **Fail at the edge** — validate env, auth, and input early; never trust client payloads
- **Replaceability** — UI and infra must be swappable without rewriting business rules
- **No silent failure** — typed errors / HTTP codes; do not swallow exceptions empty
- **Secure by default** — httpOnly cookies, no secrets in client, no ad-hoc storage paths

## Do / Don't

```ts
// ❌ BAD — page owns fetch, layout, form, and API URL strings
// ✅ GOOD — page composes features; feature uses api-client; primitives are dumb UI
```

- Prefer many small files with clear names over one “utils/helpers.ts” dumping ground
- Delete dead code in the same PR; do not leave “tmp”, commented-out blocks, or unused exports
- Optimize with evidence (measure / known hot path). Readable first; micro-opts second
